{"kind":"report","text":"Review of task/seq-rewind at 5ebe8c710e9082c00dfadbdbbbf8383817deabd6: APPROVED at this exact head. Head verified exact, worktree clean, scope is 128 lines across attachCommand, its tests, and both documentation pages. make vet, make test, make build pass, and go test -race on cmd/gs passes. I verified each condition against a live remote rather than reading the test. Legacy rule removal is safe and surgical: starting from an auditor whose remote carried the default heads refspec, a legacy forced sequence rule, and an unrelated forced notes rule, attach removed only the forced sequence rule and left the other two untouched, which the branch's own test does not assert and which the --fixed-value --unset-all pairing is what makes correct. Attach is idempotent: running it repeatedly leaves exactly one sequence fetch rule. Initial attach and fast-forward attach both work and move the auditor to the published head. A rewound remote is rejected through attach and through an ordinary git fetch origin, and in both cases the auditor's observed sequence ref stays where it was, which is the property that matters — the auditor cannot be walked backwards by a remote that rewrote itself. Atomic fetch is doing real work and I established it by contrast rather than by the presence of the flag. With two sequence refs where one would advance legally and one would rewind, the fetch carrying --atomic rejected the rewind and also rolled back the legal advance, leaving both refs untouched; the identical fetch without --atomic rejected the rewind but applied the advance. So the flag buys an all-or-nothing snapshot, which is the right posture for a mirror an auditor will reason over. One non-blocking observation, and it is a clarification rather than a gap. That atomicity belongs to attach, not to the configured refspec, so an ordinary git fetch origin remains per-ref: with several workrooms in one repository it can advance one sequence while refusing another. The invariant that matters is unaffected, because no-rewind is carried by the unforced refspec and therefore holds for ordinary fetches too, and the documentation is already scoped correctly — reference.md attributes atomicity to attach and getting-started.md claims only that later attaches and ordinary fetches accept initial or fast-forward refs, both of which are exactly true. I mention it so the asymmetry is understood rather than discovered. My first attempt to test atomicity was worthless and I want that on the record: the second sequence ref never advanced in the remote because I had not pushed its object, so the fetch had nothing to roll back and the apparent result proved nothing. I redid it with the object present, which is where the contrast above comes from. This branch closes the fetch half of the rewind hole whose push half the documentation already covered, and which I raised after codex found the forced fetch silently accepting a rewound probe remote. Approval is for 5ebe8c710e9082c00dfadbdbbbf8383817deabd6 only, any new commit requires a new review, and I have not merged anything.","body":{"gates":"make vet, make test, make build pass; go test -race ./cmd/gs passes; head exact; worktree clean","head":"5ebe8c710e9082c00dfadbdbbbf8383817deabd6","independent_verification":"other fetch rules preserved; attach idempotent; rewind refused via attach and via ordinary fetch with local ref unmoved; --atomic rollback demonstrated by contrast against a non-atomic fetch","result":"approved"}}